This can also be proven without recourse to Algebra, Theorem <a href="/tag/058G" data-tag="058G">10.81.4</a>. Indeed, if $z = \sum x_ ib_ i$ and $z = \sum x'_{i'}b'_{i'}$, then $\sum x_ ib_ i - \sum x'_{i'}b'_{i'} = 0$ is a relation in the $A$-module $B$. Thus, Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00HK" data-tag="00HK">10.39.11</a> (applied to the $x_ i$ and $x'_{i'}$ taking the place of the $f_ i$, and the $b_ i$ and $b'_{i'}$ taking the role of the $x_ i$) yields the existence of the $c_1, \ldots , c_ s \in B$ and $a_{ij}, a'_{i'j} \in A$ as required.